  "note": "Independent rejudgment after canonical subscriber-ID fixture migration. RFC 2865 Section 4.1: 'An Access-Request MUST NOT contain both a User-Password and a CHAP-Password.' The full row also requires User-Password, CHAP-Password or State and explicitly permits an extension credential instead. All attached tests were read: subscriber PAP and CHAP packets carry their credentials; no-auth and short MS-CHAPv2 inputs put no request on the wire; valid MS-CHAPv2 carries its VSA and neither base credential. The unchanged admin PAP unit excludes CHAP-Password, and the EAP conversation requires EAP-Message without either base credential on every captured request and State after the initial request. Valid credentials and signed mock replies keep transport rejection from masking these assertions. Canonical IDs identify the original subscriber cases without changing methods, credential bytes, no-request checks or captured packet assertions. This retains the existing whole-row judgment, including its extension and exclusivity clauses. Native discrimination refresh remains separate main-thread evidence.",
